WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.724
"Is FDA-Approved Bioidentical Hormone
Replacement Therapy Safe?"

00:00:06.724 --> 00:00:09.780
The news that
menopausal hormone therapy

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.120
had the potential to cause
higher rates of breast cancer,

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.760
cardiovascular disease,
and overall harm

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.700
rocked women and physicians
across the country.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.240
The study arm used progesterone
and Premarin,

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.000
because it was the most commonly
prescribed form of estrogen

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:24.960
and is still prescribed

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.300
more than a million times
a year in the U.S.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.540
It's a mixture of more
than 50 different estrogens

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:31.740
from horse pee.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.280
("Premarin" comes from the words
"pregnant mare urine,"

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.420
if you're skeptical,

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.180
try crushing a pill
and sniffing it.)

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.960
The dour findings
of the Women's Health Initiative

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.900
(combined with some high-profile
celebrity endorsements)

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.960
saw interest shift
to "bioidentical hormones,"

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.480
made from plants
rather than a horse source.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.420
They are marketed as being safer
and more effective.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.060
No wonder estimates
peg the annual number

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.400
of prescriptions
at 30 million a year,

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.100
constituting
a billion-dollar industry.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.300
One reason patients may believe
compounded hormone therapy

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.640
is safer is that,
thanks to a legal loophole,

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.440
they are not mandated
to provide

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.260
the FDA insert required
for commercial products

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.000
warning about side effects.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.040
Purchased at regular pharmacies,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.140
hormones have
a prominent black box

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.260
with an all-caps
"WARNING: ENDOMETRIAL CANCER,

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.120
CARDIOVASCULAR DISORDERS,
BREAST CANCER,

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.500
AND PROBABLE DEMENTIA."

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.920
There's no evidence
that bioidentical hormones

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:29.880
are any safer,

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.220
but the missing laundry list
of side effects

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.140
may give patients
a false sense of security.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.740
Another appeal
is customized dosing

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.020
based on saliva hormone testing,

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.740
but such tests are considered
to be unreliable

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.780
and so recommended against,

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.880
since the only people
who profit

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.460
are the ones
trying to sell you the tests.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.720
Custom-compounded hormones
are not FDA-approved,

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.180
have not been shown to be safer
or more effective,

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.140
and may lack standardization
of quality, purity, and dose.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.080
When the FDA
or muckraking journalists

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.460
have filled
the same prescriptions

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.440
in compounding pharmacies
in different states,

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.440
a third of the stated
estrogen contents

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.500
were off
by as much as 250 percent.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.980
No wonder all the major
American medical societies

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.500
that counsel physicians
on treating menopausal women

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.540
recommend against
compounded hormones,

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.000
including the American College
of Obstetrics and Gynecology,

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.620
the Endocrine Society,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.660
and the North American
Menopause Society.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.620
Characterizing the pushback
they got from their 2020 review

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.380
recommending against the use
of bioidentical hormones,

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.540
the National Academies
of Sciences,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.280
Engineering,
and Medicine concluded,

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.100
"It is clear
from these communications

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.260
that many clinicians,
compounding pharmacists,

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.660
and patients hold minimum,
if any,

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.940
concerns regarding
the medication's safety

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.020
and effectiveness."

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.180
Now, as of 2019,

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.960
there are pee-free FDA-approved
bioidentical hormones

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.360
that skirt
the standardization concerns

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.620
of compounded preparations,

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.100
but that just brings us back
to square one,

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.120
since they are considered

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.100
to have
the same risk/benefit profile.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.820
The multibillion-dollar
compounded hormone industry

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.880
is considered a "leading source
of disinformation,"

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.940
but the same could be said
of Big Pharma.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.580
With so many billions of dollars
at stake,

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:18.900
it's hard to know
who to trust.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.680
When in doubt,
I turn to The Medical Letter

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.000
on Drugs and Therapeutics,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.460
considered one
of the least biased sources

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:26.940
in medicine.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.460
It's kind of like
the Consumer Reports

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.680
of the drug world,
and, in fact,

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.840
was actually co-founded
by the co-founder

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.340
of the publisher
of Consumer Reports

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.320
more than 50 years ago.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.320
As The Medical Letter
used to brag on their website,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.020
it "does not accept grants,
from any source;

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:46.200
donations, from anyone;
[or] funding, from any entity."

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.260
They won't let their work be
used for promotional purposes,

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.180
and they don't accept
any advertising.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.820
In their review of the evidence,
they concluded,

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.160
"There is no acceptable evidence

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.800
that bio-identical hormones
are safer."

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.800
So, how do you treat
menopausal symptoms

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:02.880
like hot flashes?

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.640
The American College of
Obstetricians and Gynecologists

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.060
suggests palliative measures,
such as "consuming cool drinks,"

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.360
but is there really no way
to treat hot flashes

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.240
without the cancer, clots,
and coronaries?

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.220
Thankfully there is,

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.560
as I cover in a video series
that starts with

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.260
Menopausal Hot Flashes
Are Not Inevitable

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:25.080
and then moves
from prevention to treatment.

